From 5378c88b5b1feba3d5b732e33dcd0f1752a9c070 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dsv4-a5 <soc.yuan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 08:45:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] [feat](kt-kernel): allocate CPU MXFP4 experts only for
 the offloaded subset

kt-kernel allocated a BufferB for every one of `num_experts` experts on the
CPU regardless of `gpu_experts_mask` -- `should_skip_expert()` was a
routing-time check only, and the allocation loop in `moe_base.hpp` had no mask
test. On DeepSeek-V4-Flash that is 43 layers x 256 experts x 15.0 MiB = 162 GiB
of resident host memory, which does not fit; the single-card A5 plan only needs
~96 of 256 experts on the CPU.

Make the mask describe CPU *residency* as well as routing, opt-in via the new
`GeneralMOEConfig::skip_gpu_expert_weights` (default false, so no behaviour
change for any other backend or model). `num_experts` is deliberately NOT
reduced: every per-expert structure stays indexed by logical expert id, so an
arbitrary non-prefix residency mask works with no renumbering on either side
and the forward hot path is untouched.

  - operators/common.hpp: add `skip_gpu_expert_weights`, `is_gpu_expert()`,
    `lacks_cpu_weights()`; `should_skip_expert()` now reuses `is_gpu_expert()`
    (semantics unchanged).
  - operators/amx/moe_base.hpp: skip the three `aligned_alloc` BufferB
    allocations for masked experts. The BufferA/BufferC handles own no memory
    (they are re-pointed into the shared pools each forward) and stay in place.
  - operators/amx/fp4-moe.hpp: skip masked experts in the three
    `AMX_FP4_MOE_TP::load_weights` jobs and in both staging-copy loops of
    `TP_MOE<AMX_FP4_MOE_TP>::load_weights` (the staging array is
    default-initialised, so untouched pages never become resident);
    `write_weight_scale_to_buffer` now throws instead of dereferencing a null
    BufferB for an expert with no CPU weights.
  - python/utils/loader.py: `MXFP4SafeTensorLoader.load_experts(expert_ids=...)`
    reads only the requested checkpoint experts and leaves the other slots
    None, keeping the returned lists indexable by logical id.
  - python/experts_base.py: `BaseMoEWrapper.cpu_expert_ids()`.
  - python/utils/amx.py: pass the subset (mapped through
    physical_to_logical_map), tolerate None pointer slots, set the C++ flag
    only when a subset was really taken, and release the loader's temporaries
    with `del weights` + `malloc_trim(0)` -- without that glibc retains ~5 MiB
    per expert in its arenas (20.2 vs 15.1 resident MiB/expert, i.e. 22 GiB of
    pure fragmentation at E=96 x 43 layers).

Verified on DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731 layer 3 against an independent float32
dequantisation of the same safetensors bytes (E2M1 x 2^e8m0, clamped SwiGLU
limit 10.0), with a non-prefix mask {0,2,4,6} resident:

  tokens=1/8/64   relative Frobenius 3.9e-03 / 3.9e-03 / 3.8e-03,
                  per-token cosine >= 0.999991  (bf16 rounding only)
  subset off      bit-identical to the pre-change results

Measured resident memory for one layer, 256 logical experts:

  offloaded  8 -> 140.6 MiB, 32 -> 501.4, 96 -> 1462.1, 256 -> 3863.9
  marginal   15.014 MiB/expert/layer  (predicted n*k*0.625 = 15.0)
  projected  43 layers: E=64 40.6 GiB, E=96 60.8 GiB, E=128 81.1 GiB

